{"conversation":"topic-switch","question":"How do flaps affect lift?","reply":{"answer":"High-lift devices, such as flaps, exist to increase CLmax and reduce the stall speed.","ids":["/manual/flight-fundamentals/principles-of-flight/#axes"],"topic":[],"references":[{"id":"/manual/flight-fundamentals/principles-of-flight/#axes","title":"Fixed-Wing Principles of Flight · Axes and flight controls"}]},"ms":1301,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"topic-switch","question":"Tell me about radar","reply":{"answer":"Pulse-Doppler radar measures range by timing and closure by Doppler shift, using the shift to reject clutter and track targets in velocity. Pulse repetition frequency (PRF) determines how far a radar can measure range unambiguously and its velocity measurement capability, with higher PRFs providing excellent velocity resolution but causing range ambiguity. Additionally, radar correlation involves matching a radar contact to a controller's call by placing the cursor and scan volume at the specified bearing, range, and altitude.","ids":["/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar","/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-correlation","/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#recap","/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#prf"],"topic":[],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar","title":"Pulse-Doppler radar"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-correlation","title":"Radar correlation"},{"id":"/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#recap","title":"Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures · Key points"},{"id":"/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#prf","title":"Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures · PRF and its trade-offs"}]},"ms":2277,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"comparison","question":"What tower frequencies do Sochi and Kutaisi use?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Caucasus, Sochi-Adler uses tower frequencies VHF 127.0, UHF 256.0, low VHF 39.6, and HF 4.05, while Kutaisi uses VHF 134.0, UHF 263.0, low VHF 41.0, and HF 4.4.","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-kutaisi"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/","/encyclopedia/locations/kutaisi/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","title":"Sochi-Adler · DCS: Caucasus"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-kutaisi","title":"Kutaisi · DCS: Caucasus"}]},"ms":1969,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"comparison","question":"Compare the engines of the F-14 and F-16.","reply":{"answer":"The F-14 Tomcat uses two afterburning turbofans, either Pratt & Whitney TF30-P-414A (20,900 lb each) for the F-14A or General Electric F110-GE-400 (27,000 lb each) for the F-14B/D. In contrast, the F-16 Fighting Falcon is a single-engine aircraft using either a General Electric F110-GE-129 (29,500 lbf) on the Block 50 or a Pratt & Whitney F100-PW-229 on the Block 52.","ids":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/#swing-wings-and-phoenix-missiles","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","title":"F-14 Tomcat"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/#swing-wings-and-phoenix-missiles","title":"F-14 Tomcat · Swing wings and Phoenix missiles"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/","title":"F-16 Fighting Falcon"}]},"ms":3018,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"long-context","question":"What is the VORTAC for Ramat David?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Sinai, the VORTAC is RMD, channel 84X, 113.7 MHz; in DCS: Syria, the VORTAC is RMD, channel 84X, 113.7 MHz.","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":1353,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"long-context","question":"What runways are available?","reply":{"answer":"The available runways at Ramat David Airbase are 09/27, 11/29, and 15/33 (source 1). In DCS: Sinai, the runways are 09/27, 11/29, and 15/33 (source 3), while in DCS: Syria, they are 09/27, 11/29, and 33/15 (source 4).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/","title":"Ramat David Airbase"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":2352,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"long-context","question":"Which one runs east-west?","reply":{"answer":"None of the runways at Ramat David Airbase run east-west; runway 09/27 has headings of 082° and 262°, runway 11/29 has headings of 103° and 283°, and runway 15/33 has headings of 140° and 320° (source 2, source 3).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":2830,"passed":false,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"long-context","question":"What about tower frequency?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Sinai, the tower frequencies are VHF 118.0 MHz and UHF 251.1 MHz (source 3). In DCS: Syria, the tower frequencies are VHF 118.6 MHz and UHF 251.3 MHz (source 4).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":1906,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
{"conversation":"long-context","question":"And the VORTAC channel again?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Sinai, the VORTAC RMD is on channel 84X and 113.7 MHz (source 3). In DCS: Syria, the VORTAC RMD is also on channel 84X and 113.7 MHz (source 4).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":777,"passed":true,"revision":"019e99d4c61b60d5a44c350656fa28e76dbad556"}
